    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescopes</a> he built himself, Galileo saw the Moon's mountains, Jupiter's four largest moons and the phases of Venus, observations that made the Ptolemaic model untenable.</li><li>He joined experiment and mathematics in the study of motion, describing uniform acceleration in falling bodies, inertia and the principle of relativity that later underlay Newton's laws.</li><li>His defence of Copernican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heliocentrism">heliocentrism</a> brought an order to abandon it in 1616 and, after his Dialogue of 1632, a sentence as 'vehemently suspect of heresy' and house arrest for the rest of his life.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a>' was coined for Galileo's instrument at a banquet in 1611, when he was made a member of Prince Federico Cesi's Accademia dei Lincei; a fellow member coined '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">microscope</a>' for another of his instruments a few years later.</p></aside>
    </section>

